The original is a Hermes Agent plugin in Python: a PRE_LLM_CALL hook scores
each user message against eight keyword rules, moves a mood (upset to happy,
calm to lively), a rapport level and three slow traits (warmth, playfulness,
directness) within the original's caps, drifts the mood back toward baseline
by half its distance every six hours, and appends one line of about thirty
tokens to that message; /mood, /mood reset, /mood off and /mood on show and
steer it, and the state is a JSON file per Hermes profile. This rewrite keeps
the same rules, the same numbers (baseline 0.1 and 0.5, a 0.35 cap on one
message's shift, traits between 0.1 and 0.9 moving 0.02 a signal, a six-hour
half-life, rapport that only creeps up by 0.003 a turn), the same words the
line is built from, the same mention of a break of an hour or more on the
first turn after it, and the same three commands behind /mood. The seams are
Nodecode's: the line is recorded into the request about to be built at the
:ROUND point rather than appended to a user message, the state is a JSON file
under the nodecode home (one per profile, written atomically), and the verbs
are callable from eval as (mood:show), (mood:reset) and (mood:set-enabled t).

Left out, because the harness differs: the hook's multimodal branch (a Hermes
message can arrive as parts; a Nodecode turn's words are one string), and the
one-time migration of a state file a version 1.0 kept beside the module (a
Nodecode cell never kept state there). Added: the reading half of the line can
drop the original's "don't mention it unless asked" (the `quiet` setting), the
file can be moved with `state_file`, and the half-life is a setting instead of
a constant.
